<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montgenèvre Pass</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Col de Montgenèvre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Montgenèvre Pass is a high pass in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottian-alps">Cottian Alps</a> linking the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durance">Durance</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In antiquity it carried a main Roman road from Italy into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, through lands ruled by the Alpine king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-julius-cottius">Cottius</a> and his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donnus">Donnus</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was one of the crossings that gave the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> their strategic weight.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montgenevre-pass.jpg" alt="Montgenèvre Pass" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francofranco56 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colle_del_Monginevro.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
